---
import SabGuide from '../../components/sab/SabGuide.astro';
import { getGuide } from '../../lib/guides';

const guide = getGuide('chemistry-competitions-canada');
---

<SabGuide guide={guide}>
  <p>
    Chemistry competitions in Canada look like several separate contests and are really one ladder with a confusing set of names. There is one entry point, and if you miss it in April there is no second door that year. This is the whole structure, in the order you meet it.
  </p>

  <h2>There is no junior chemistry olympiad</h2>
  <p>
    Start here, because it is the most common wrong assumption. People search for a "Junior Canadian Chemistry Olympiad" every month, and the Chemical Institute of Canada, which runs both the contest and the olympiad, publishes no junior division of either. There is no Grade 9 or Grade 10 track.
  </p>
  <p>
    That is not the same as saying a younger student can do nothing. The eligibility bar is <em>taking chemistry</em> and being under 20, not being in a particular grade. A Grade 10 student in Chemistry 20 can write the contest. Most do not, and most who do score badly, which is fine: the paper is the best possible study guide for writing it seriously a year later. If you want a competition built for your age instead, look at <a href="/programs/">the science fair and olympiad listings</a>, several of which start in Grade 9.
  </p>

  <h2>The ladder, in order</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>The Canadian Chemistry Contest (CCC)</strong> is the entry point and the only one. It is aimed at the top 10% of Canadian high school and cégep students under 20 who are taking chemistry. You write it at your own school.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The <a href="/programs/canadian-chemistry-olympiad-cco/">Canadian Chemistry Olympiad (CCO)</a></strong> is not a separate entry. It runs in conjunction with the contest and is aimed at the top 5%. You reach it by doing well on the CCC; there is no form to fill in.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The National Camp</strong> takes the strongest CCO performers and prepares a team.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The International Chemistry Olympiad (IChO)</strong> is where that team competes for Canada.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    So the practical instruction is short: the only thing you actually have to do is get registered for the CCC. Everything above it is selection, not application.
  </p>

  <h2>The date that decides your year</h2>
  <p>
    For the most recent published cycle, the Chemical Institute of Canada set the contest for <strong>Wednesday, April 22, 2026</strong>, with registration closing at <strong>noon EDT on Tuesday, March 31, 2026</strong>. The CIC had not published 2027 dates when this was written, but the shape has been stable: register by the end of March, write in late April.
  </p>
  <p>
    The registration deadline is the part that catches people, because <strong>you do not register yourself</strong>. Sign-up runs through a regional or district coordinator, which in practice means a teacher at your school has to enter you. If nobody at your school has heard of the contest, nobody enters, and no student there writes it that year. That is the single most common reason an eligible Alberta student never competes.
  </p>

  <h2>What to actually do</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Ask your chemistry teacher in February</strong> whether your school writes the CCC, and if the answer is no, ask them to find the regional coordinator. Six weeks of notice is enough; two is not.</li>
    <li><strong>Work through past papers.</strong> The CIC points at International Chemistry Olympiad preparatory problems as the model for the harder questions, so that is what to practise on once the curriculum questions feel easy.</li>
    <li><strong>Do not wait for Grade 12.</strong> Writing it once in Grade 11 with no expectations is how the students who place in Grade 12 got there.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>If you get to the olympiad round</h2>
  <p>
    Selection for the National Camp is scored across three pieces rather than one exam: the problem sets are worth 5%, a take-home exam 15%, and the CCO exam itself 80%, with the camp taken from roughly the top 10% on the combined score.
  </p>
  <p>
    One rule inside that is worth reading twice: <strong>a student who does not complete the take-home exam is not eligible for the National Camp or IChO at all</strong>, regardless of their exam mark. It is 15% of the score and a hard gate on the other 85%. If you reach this round, the take-home is the piece to finish first.
  </p>

  <h2>The neighbouring ladders</h2>
  <p>
    Biology and physics are organised the same way, with a qualifying exam feeding a national team, and the qualifiers land in a similar window, so a student who likes one often writes two:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong><a href="/programs/canadian-biology-olympiad-cbo/">Canadian Biology Olympiad</a></strong>: national exam in early April, then a training camp for those selected.</li>
    <li><strong><a href="/programs/canadian-physics-olympiad-cpho/">Canadian Physics Olympiad</a></strong>: you qualify by writing the <a href="/programs/cap-high-school-prize-exam/">CAP High School Prize Exam</a>, usually in mid-April.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    All three qualifiers cluster in April, which is also when Grade 12 diploma prep starts in earnest. Deciding in February which ones you are writing is a much better plan than discovering the overlap in the second week of April.
  </p>

  <h2>Why bother</h2>
  <p>
    Competition results are one of the few things on a scholarship application that are not self-reported. "Placed in the Canadian Chemistry Olympiad" is checkable in a way that "passionate about science" is not, and the awards that reward demonstrated work rather than an essay, several of them on <a href="/scholarships/stem/">the STEM scholarships page</a>, are exactly the ones that ask for evidence of this kind. The <a href="/guides/high-school-research-programs-alberta/">research programs guide</a> covers the other half of that evidence.
  </p>
  <p class="sabg-note">
    Dates, eligibility and the National Camp weighting here are from the Chemical Institute of Canada and the Chemistry Olympiad of Canada, checked on September 1, 2026. The CIC had not posted 2027 dates at that point, so confirm the current cycle on <a href="https://www.cheminst.ca/discover/canadian-chemistry-contest/" rel="noopener">the CIC contest page</a> before you plan around it.
  </p>
</SabGuide>
